#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""lint-tasknames — assert a repo's runnable task names follow the shared vocabulary.

Reads the category list FROM the deployed naming-build-tasks skill (single source;
this tool deliberately carries no copy of the vocabulary), then reads every task
name a repo declares through five readers — `just --summary`, package.json
scripts, `make -qp`, Nx (`nx show project --json` per project) and mise
(`mise tasks ls --json`) — and checks each name for:

  separator  the name carries a colon (the shape is `verb-qualifier`, hyphen)
  verb       the first hyphen-separated token is not a vocabulary category

Read-only. Exit 0 clean, 1 violations, 2 a READER ERROR (a build file exists but
its reader could not enumerate tasks — reported loudly and separately, because a
reader that silently reads nothing would be a green that means nothing).

Discovery is TREE-WIDE for just/npm/make/mise: any nested justfile / package.json /
Makefile / mise config is read too (node_modules, .git, dist and similar are pruned), because a
single-directory read once hid an entire frontend/package.json of 32 scripts and
under-reported a repo's true naming scope by half. Nx stays root-only on purpose:
the workspace graph already enumerates every nested project. Findings from a
nested dir are labeled with its relative path. Test-fixture directories (`fixture`,
`fixtures`, `__fixtures__`, and names starting `fixture-` or `fixture_`) are not walked:
their build files are test input, not this repository's tasks. The header counts
them. A build tool with no marker file
anywhere in the tree is a skip, not an error; the per-source tally line makes
skips visible so coverage is never implied.

Commit gate. `lint-tasknames --staged [REPO]` (REPO defaults to the current directory and is
resolved to the git top level) lists the staged paths (`git diff --cached --name-only
--diff-filter=ACMR -z`) and lints only when one is a file a reader parses: a just, npm, make or Nx
build file (`nx.json` at the root, `project.json` at any depth), a mise config or a file under a mise task directory (derived from the readers' own
constants, so it cannot drift from them), never one inside a directory the walk skips (fixtures,
`.worktrees/`, `.claude/`, node_modules-class directories). With none staged it prints
`lint-tasknames: no task file staged` and exits 0. Otherwise it runs the normal lint with gate
semantics, 1 on any ERROR, 2 on a reader or usage error (not a git repository, git failing, `--staged`
with `--fleet`), 0 otherwise (a WARN never blocks), and on a non-zero exit adds a final line saying the
commit is refused, naming skill `naming-build-tasks`, which holds the vocabulary. The lint reads the WORKING
TREE: a partial `git add` of a build file is judged as it stands on disk, not as staged. The hook is
one line, `lint-tasknames --staged || exit 1`, and the hook itself must refuse when the tool is
missing, since a missing tool is a command-not-found the hook would otherwise skip.

Allowances. A domain may list, in ONE allowlist file, names a project did not choose because they
mirror an upstream tool's own subcommands. The file is `$LINT_TASKNAMES_ALLOWANCES`, else
`~/.dotlocal/lint-tasknames/allowances.toml`; with neither there are no allowances and the run
header says so. One `[[allowance]]` table per entry: `repo` (the manifest's project name, which is
the same on every machine; a linked worktree or a subdirectory run is matched to its main checkout),
`path` (optional: the build file the names live in, relative to the repository root, normalized, with
no `..`; for Nx the build file is always `nx.json`), `mirror` (the upstream tool), `surface` (the
command that proves it), `names` (non-empty list) and `reason`. A covered name is reported INFO
instead of WARN; an allowance never lowers an ERROR (a colon, a triad misuse). An allowance naming no
current task is a stale allowance (WARN).

The surface is the mirrored tool itself: its first word must equal `mirror` and carry no slash, so a
repository's own script, or the build tool being linted, cannot vouch for its own names. Under
--strict only, each surface for the repository being linted is run in that repository's top
directory, with no shell, stdin closed, NO_COLOR=1, a timeout and an output cap; ANSI escapes are
stripped, and every claimed name must appear in the output (stdout and stderr) as a whole word. A
surface that fails, times out, exceeds the cap or is not found is an ERROR. RESIDUAL, stated plainly:
a name that merely appears in the tool's prose passes, so the writer's review of each allowance
remains the control. A malformed allowlist is exit 2.

Ecosystem-mandated names are exempt, not violations: npm lifecycle scripts
(prepare, install, version, …, and pre<X>/post<X> of another script), npm's
reserved `start`/`test` (kept as thin delegating aliases by convention), and
just's `default` listing idiom, and mise's `default`, the task `mise run` runs with no name.

mise. A directory is read when it holds a mise config or task directory (`mise.toml`, `mise-tasks/`
and mise's other spellings of both). `mise tasks ls --json --hidden` there also lists the tasks of
every config ABOVE it (a parent directory's `mise.toml`, the global config), so only a task whose
`source` lies inside that directory counts: the repository's own. Hidden tasks count, because `hide`
only keeps a task out of mise's listing; it is still run by name. Aliases are not linted, as just's
are not. A file task under a subdirectory of `mise-tasks/` gets mise's `group:name` name, which the
separator rule reports. A config mise cannot parse, or a missing `mise`, is a READER ERROR. Listing
needs no trust for a plain config (measured with mise 2026.11.14), though mise records each config it
read in its own state directory. A config with `[vars]` or any `{{ ... }}` template is different:
mise refuses to list it untrusted ("are not trusted", exit 1). Trusting it is not an option, because
listing a TRUSTED config evaluates its `[vars]` templates, `exec(...)` included (a template in a
task's `run` is not evaluated by listing; both measured 2026.11.14), so trust (even per process)
would let a committed mise config run code at commit time. So when `mise tasks ls` fails with "not trusted",
that directory is read STATICALLY, running nothing: each config file in `MISE_MARKERS` is parsed with
tomllib (a TOML syntax error stays a READER ERROR) and the keys of its top-level `tasks` table are the
task names (a value is the run string or a table; hidden tasks count). The inline-task `shell` rule
below applies to those tasks from the same fields. The header names each directory read this way
("untrusted, read without mise"), so the coverage is visible. Limits, each stated in the header when
it applies: a static read does not follow `task_config.includes`, and it does not read file tasks
(mise-tasks/ and its other spellings), because mise's naming of them (extension stripping, which
measured 2026.11.14 does for `.sh`, `.py`, `.js` and not for `.tar` or `.md`, and drops `.toml`
files) is not reproduced here. Any other `mise tasks ls` failure is still a READER ERROR.

One mise rule is not about the name. An inline task (a `run` in a mise config, not a task file, whose
shebang chooses its interpreter) must name its own `shell`, and a POSIX shell there must set
pipefail (`bash -euo pipefail -c`); otherwise it is an ERROR (`shell`). mise ignores
`unix_default_inline_shell_args` in a project's mise.toml, so a task without its own `shell` runs
without pipefail and `false | true` succeeds (measured with mise 2026.11.5).
"""

import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import tomllib
from pathlib import Path

SKILL_CANDIDATES = [
    Path.home() / ".claude/skills/naming-build-tasks/SKILL.md",
    Path.home() / ".local/share/chezmoi/dot_claude/skills/naming-build-tasks/SKILL.md",
]

NPM_LIFECYCLE = {
    "prepare", "prepublish", "prepublishOnly", "prepack", "postpack",
    "preinstall", "install", "postinstall", "preuninstall", "uninstall",
    "preversion", "version", "postversion", "dependencies",
}
NPM_RESERVED_ALIASES = {"start", "test"}
JUST_IDIOM = {"default"}
MISE_IDIOM = {"default"}
# Targets Nx itself (or its plugins) provides on inferred projects — machinery,
# not authored names, and inference-first is the stated preference, so
# these are ecosystem-mandated the same way npm lifecycle scripts are.
NX_PROVIDED = {"nx-release-publish", "watch-deps"}

# --- severity ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The point of the split: a leading token outside the vocabulary (`wifi-capture`)
# confuses nobody, while using `check` for something that verifies is the confusion the triad exists
# to prevent -- and treating them at identical weight is what trained readers to skim this tool.
ERROR, WARN, INFO = "ERROR", "WARN", "INFO"
STRICT = False

# --- allowances ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALLOWANCE_KEYS = {"repo", "path", "mirror", "surface", "names", "reason"}
ALLOWANCE_REQUIRED = ("repo", "mirror", "surface", "names", "reason")
SURFACE_TIMEOUT = 10.0  # seconds a surface command may run; LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_TIMEOUT overrides (tests)
SURFACE_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 20  # output a surface may print before it is killed; LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_MAX_BYTES overrides (tests)
ANSI_RE = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;?]*[ -/]*[@-~]")
DEFAULT_ALLOWANCES = Path.home() / ".dotlocal/lint-tasknames/allowances.toml"
DEFAULT_MANIFEST = Path.home() / "Devel" / "mani.yaml"
ALLOWANCES = None       # (file, [allowance dict]) when a file was read, else None
ALLOWANCES_LOOKED_AT = None

def allowance_error(msg):
    print(f"lint-tasknames: allowances: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


def load_allowances():
    """Read the allowlist; sets ALLOWANCES. A malformed file is exit 2, never a quiet no-op."""
    global ALLOWANCES, ALLOWANCES_LOOKED_AT
    env = os.environ.get("LINT_TASKNAMES_ALLOWANCES")
    path = Path(env) if env else DEFAULT_ALLOWANCES
    ALLOWANCES_LOOKED_AT = path
    if not path.is_file():
        if env:  # an explicitly named file that is missing is a mistake, not "no allowances"
            allowance_error(f"{path} (from LINT_TASKNAMES_ALLOWANCES) does not exist")
        ALLOWANCES = None
        return
    try:
        data = tomllib.loads(path.read_text())
    except (tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        allowance_error(f"{path} unreadable: {e}")
    extra = set(data) - {"allowance"}
    if extra:
        allowance_error(f"{path}: unknown top-level key(s) {sorted(extra)}; only [[allowance]] tables are read")
    entries = data.get("allowance", [])
    if not isinstance(entries, list):
        allowance_error(f"{path}: `allowance` must be an array of tables ([[allowance]])")
    out = []
    for i, a in enumerate(entries, 1):
        label = f"allowance #{i}" + (f" ({a.get('mirror')})" if isinstance(a, dict) and isinstance(a.get("mirror"), str) else "")
        if not isinstance(a, dict):
            allowance_error(f"{label}: must be a table")
        for k in sorted(set(a) - ALLOWANCE_KEYS):
            allowance_error(f"{label}: unknown key '{k}'")
        for k in ALLOWANCE_REQUIRED:
            if k not in a:
                allowance_error(f"{label}: missing key '{k}'")
        for k in ("repo", "mirror", "surface", "reason", "path"):
            if k in a and (not isinstance(a[k], str) or not a[k].strip()):
                allowance_error(f"{label}: key '{k}' must be a non-empty string")
        if not isinstance(a["names"], list) or not a["names"] or not all(isinstance(n, str) and n.strip() for n in a["names"]):
            allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'names' must be a non-empty list of non-empty strings")
        try:
            argv = shlex.split(a["surface"])
        except ValueError as e:
            allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'surface' is not a parseable command: {e}")
        if "path" in a:
            pth = a["path"]
            if Path(pth).is_absolute() or ".." in Path(pth).parts or pth == "." or os.path.normpath(pth) != pth:
                allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'path' must be a normalized path relative to the repository root "
                                "(no leading './', '//', trailing '/', or '..')")
        if not argv:
            allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'surface' is empty")
        if "/" in argv[0]:
            allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'surface' must name the mirrored tool from PATH, not a path "
                            f"('{argv[0]}'): a repository's own script cannot be its own surface")
        if argv[0] != a["mirror"]:
            allowance_error(f"{label}: key 'surface' must run the mirrored tool: its first word is "
                            f"'{argv[0]}', not '{a['mirror']}'")
        out.append(dict(a, _label=label, _argv=argv))
    ALLOWANCES = (path, out)


def env_number(name, default):
    """A positive number from the environment, else `default`; anything else is exit 2, not a traceback."""
    raw = os.environ.get(name)
    if raw is None:
        return default
    try:
        v = float(raw)
    except ValueError:
        v = 0
    if not v > 0:
        print(f"lint-tasknames: {name}={raw!r} is not a positive number", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    return v


def kill_group(proc):
    try:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
    except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
        proc.kill()


def verify_surface(a, repo):
    """Run one allowance's surface in `repo`; return a list of ERROR reason strings (empty = verified)."""
    timeout = env_number("LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_TIMEOUT", SURFACE_TIMEOUT)
    cap = int(env_number("LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_MAX_BYTES", SURFACE_MAX_BYTES))
    what = f"surface `{a['surface']}`"
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(a["_argv"], cwd=repo, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, env=dict(os.environ, NO_COLOR="1"),
                                start_new_session=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return [f"{what} was not found"]
    except OSError as e:
        return [f"{what} could not run: {e}"]
    buf, over = bytearray(), []

    def pump():
        while True:
            chunk = proc.stdout.read1(65536)
            if not chunk:
                return
            buf.extend(chunk)
            if len(buf) > cap:
                over.append(True)
                kill_group(proc)
                return

    t = threading.Thread(target=pump, daemon=True)
    t.start()
    t.join(timeout)
    try:
        if t.is_alive():
            kill_group(proc)
            t.join()
            return [f"{what} timed out after {timeout:g}s"]
        if over:
            return [f"{what} printed more than {cap} bytes (mirror {a['mirror']}); killed"]
        try:
            proc.wait(timeout=timeout)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            kill_group(proc)
            return [f"{what} timed out after {timeout:g}s"]
    finally:
        kill_group(proc)
        proc.wait()
        proc.stdout.close()
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return [f"{what} exited {proc.returncode}"]
    text = ANSI_RE.sub("", buf.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    missing = [n for n in a["names"] if not re.search(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_-])" + re.escape(n) + r"(?![A-Za-z0-9_-])", text)]
    return [f"{what} output lacks the claimed name '{n}' (mirror {a['mirror']})" for n in missing]


# What each triad word READS, per the vocabulary skill:
#   check    the tree -- source, config, generated or built output   "is it well-formed?"
#   verify   this machine and this repo's history                    "is it actually true?"
#   validate a deployed, running target                              "is the live thing good?"
# These patterns look for unambiguous evidence of the wrong SUBJECT, never for the absence of one:
# a body with no strong signal is left alone rather than guessed at.
MACHINE_RE = re.compile(r"\b(systemctl|pacman|brew list|dpkg|rpm -q|/etc/|command -v|which |uname|"
                        r"stat |ssh-add|gpg --list|git (log|rev-parse|rev-list|show-ref|verify-commit))\b")
LIVE_RE    = re.compile(r"(https?://|\bcurl\b|\bwget\b|playwright|lighthouse|\bnc -z\b|localhost:\d)")

def triad_finding(name, body):
    """Return a reason string when a triad word is used against the wrong subject, else None."""
    head = name.split("-")[0]
    if head not in ("check", "verify", "validate") or not body:
        return None
    machine, live = bool(MACHINE_RE.search(body)), bool(LIVE_RE.search(body))
    if head == "check" and live:
        return "triad: `check` reads the TREE, but this task reaches a live target -- `validate`"
    if head == "check" and machine:
        return "triad: `check` reads the TREE, but this task inspects the MACHINE/history -- `verify`"
    if head == "verify" and live:
        return "triad: `verify` reads this machine and history, but this task reaches a live target -- `validate`"
    # Positive evidence only, as above: a `validate` that hands off to another tool (a CI trigger, a
    # runner script) shows no live signal in its own body and is still correct, so the absence of one
    # is no finding. Inspecting the machine with nothing live in sight is.
    if head == "validate" and machine and not live:
        return "triad: `validate` reads a DEPLOYED target, but this task inspects the MACHINE/history -- `verify`"
    return None


ROW = re.compile(r"^\| `([a-z0-9-]+)`")
TRIAD = re.compile(r"^\| `([a-z]+)` · `([a-z]+)` · `([a-z]+)` \|")


def load_categories():
    for cand in SKILL_CANDIDATES:
        if cand.is_file():
            cats = set()
            for line in cand.read_text().splitlines():
                m = TRIAD.match(line)
                if m:
                    cats.update(m.groups())
                    continue
                m = ROW.match(line)
                if m:
                    cats.add(m.group(1))
            if cats:
                return cats, cand
            print(f"lint-tasknames: READER ERROR: no category rows parsed from {cand}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(2)
    print("lint-tasknames: READER ERROR: naming-build-tasks skill not found "
          "(are the dotfiles applied on this machine?)", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


def run(cmd, cwd):
    return subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)


# Pruned while walking: dependency/build/output trees whose task-shaped files are
# not this repo's authored surface (a planted node_modules/package.json must never
# count), plus VCS/agent internals.
EXCLUDE_DIRS = {
    "node_modules", ".git", ".claude", ".nx", ".angular", ".venv", "venv",
    "__pycache__", "dist", "build", "out", "coverage", "vendor", "tmp",
    ".cache", ".idea", ".vscode",
    # Package-manager caches and language build sandboxes. Added 2026-09-04: a `~/Devel` run
    # produced four READER ERRORs and every one was inside a third-party tree -- a Go module cache
    # under an AUR build directory, and an upstream clone's schematics EXAMPLE whose package.json is
    # deliberately unparseable because it is a template. None of it is code we own, none of it is
    # ours to name, and their errors made the tool announce its own coverage was incomplete. A
    # reader error has to mean "a repo you own could not be enumerated" or it stops meaning anything.
    "gopath", "target", ".cargo", ".gradle", ".m2",
    # A linked worktree is a separate checkout of the same repository, never part of the main one.
    # `.worktrees` nests them; `.claude/worktrees` (the claudeWorktrees layout) is already skipped by
    # `.claude` above, so no path-aware rule is needed.
    ".worktrees",
}


# Test fixtures are input a test hands to the code under test, shaped however that test needs (a
# sample app's `test:e2e` script, a deliberately broken build file), so their task names are not this
# repository's interface and renaming them would change what the test measures. Their directories are
# not walked, and the header counts them, so the skip never reads as coverage.
FIXTURE_DIRS = {"fixture", "fixtures", "__fixtures__"}
SKIPPED_FIXTURES = set()


def is_fixture_dir(name):
    n = name.lower()
    return n in FIXTURE_DIRS or n.startswith(("fixture-", "fixture_"))


def find_dirs(repo, markers, prune=()):
    """Yield every dir under repo (repo first) containing one of the marker files, or, when
    `markers` is a function, every dir it accepts. Directories named in `prune` are not walked."""
    hits = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(repo):
        keep = []
        for d in sorted(dirnames):
            if d in EXCLUDE_DIRS or d in prune:
                continue
            if is_fixture_dir(d):
                SKIPPED_FIXTURES.add(Path(dirpath, d))
                continue
            keep.append(d)
        dirnames[:] = keep
        if markers(Path(dirpath)) if callable(markers) else any(m in filenames for m in markers):
            hits.append(Path(dirpath))
    return hits


def real_build_file(d, markers):
    """The build file's on-disk name in `d`, from a directory listing: on a case-insensitive
    filesystem `(d / "justfile").is_file()` is true for `Justfile`, and an allowance's `path` is
    matched against the name that is actually there."""
    listing = os.listdir(d)
    for m in markers:
        if m in listing:
            return m
    for m in markers:
        for real in sorted(listing):
            if real.lower() == m.lower() and (d / real).is_file():
                return real
    return markers[0]


class ReaderError(Exception):
    pass


def read_just(repo):
    if not any((repo / n).is_file() for n in ("justfile", "Justfile", ".justfile")):
        return None
    r = run(["just", "--summary"], repo)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise ReaderError(f"just --summary failed: {r.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1] if r.stderr.strip() else 'rc=' + str(r.returncode)}")
    names = r.stdout.split()
    # Recipe BODIES, for the triad check: the triad is defined by WHAT A TASK READS, so a name-only
    # reader cannot judge it at all. `just --dump` is best-effort -- a repo whose justfile does not
    # dump still gets its names linted, just not its triad usage.
    bodies = {}
    d = run(["just", "--dump"], repo)
    if d.returncode == 0:
        cur = None
        for line in d.stdout.splitlines():
            m = re.match(r"^([a-z][a-z0-9-]*)(?: [^:]*)?:", line)
            if m and not line.startswith((" ", "\t")):
                cur = m.group(1); bodies[cur] = []
            elif cur and (line.startswith(" ") or line.startswith("\t")):
                bodies[cur].append(line.strip())
    return [(n, None, " ".join(bodies.get(n, []))) for n in names]


def read_npm(repo):
    pkg = repo / "package.json"
    if not pkg.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        scripts = json.loads(pkg.read_text()).get("scripts", {})
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        raise ReaderError(f"package.json unparseable: {e}")
    names = []
    keys = set(scripts)
    for name in scripts:
        if name in NPM_LIFECYCLE or name in NPM_RESERVED_ALIASES:
            continue
        base = re.sub(r"^(pre|post)", "", name)
        if base != name and base in keys:
            continue  # pre/post hook of another script — npm-mandated shape
        names.append((name, None))
    return names


MAKE_TARGET = re.compile(r"^([^#.\s$][^:=\s]*):(?:[^=]|$)")


def read_make(repo):
    if not any((repo / n).is_file() for n in ("Makefile", "makefile", "GNUmakefile")):
        return None
    r = run(["make", "-qp"], repo)
    # make -q exits 1 for "targets not up to date" — only 2 is an error.
    if r.returncode >= 2:
        err = r.stderr.strip().splitlines()
        raise ReaderError(f"make -qp failed: {err[-1] if err else 'rc=2'}")
    names, in_db = set(), False
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("# Files"):
            in_db = True
        if not in_db:
            continue
        m = MAKE_TARGET.match(line)
        if m and "/" not in m.group(1) and m.group(1) != "Makefile":
            names.add(m.group(1))
    return [(n, None) for n in sorted(names)]


NX_MARKER = "nx.json"
# An Nx project's own build file. Unlike nx.json it sits at any depth (`web/project.json`), and the
# reader above enumerates it through `nx show projects`, so a staged one changes what the lint sees.
NX_PROJECT_FILE = "project.json"


def read_nx(repo):
    if not (repo / NX_MARKER).is_file():
        return None
    r = run(["npx", "--no-install", "nx", "show", "projects", "--json"], repo)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        err = r.stderr.strip().splitlines()
        raise ReaderError(f"nx show projects failed: {err[-1] if err else 'rc=' + str(r.returncode)}")
    names = []
    for project in json.loads(r.stdout):
        pr = run(["npx", "--no-install", "nx", "show", "project", project, "--json"], repo)
        if pr.returncode != 0:
            raise ReaderError(f"nx show project {project} failed")
        for target in json.loads(pr.stdout).get("targets", {}):
            names.append((target, project))
    return names


# Where mise finds a directory's own config and task files, relative to that directory. Any one of
# them makes the directory a mise directory. mise itself enumerates the tasks; this list only decides
# where to ask, so a spelling missing here shows as `mise(-)`, a visible skip, never a wrong count.
MISE_MARKERS = (
    "mise.toml", ".mise.toml", "mise.local.toml", ".mise.local.toml",
    ".config/mise.toml", ".config/mise/config.toml", ".config/mise/mise.toml", ".config/mise/conf.d",
    ".mise/config.toml", "mise/config.toml",
    "mise-tasks", ".mise-tasks", "mise/tasks", ".mise/tasks", ".config/mise/tasks",
)
# Those locations' own directories: a directory's config, never a project of its own, so the walk
# does not enter them (`.config/mise.toml` would otherwise make `.config` a second mise directory).
MISE_OWN_DIRS = {".config", ".mise", "mise", "mise-tasks", ".mise-tasks"}
MISE_BODY_MAX = 1 << 16  # bytes of a file task read for the triad check


def is_mise_dir(d):
    return any((d / m).exists() for m in MISE_MARKERS)


# Directories read without mise this run, as (directory, [what the static read does not cover]).
MISE_STATIC = []
MISE_TASK_DIRS = ("mise-tasks", ".mise-tasks", "mise/tasks", ".mise/tasks", ".config/mise/tasks")


def mise_config_files(d):
    """The config files among MISE_MARKERS in `d`, in the order listed (`conf.d` holds many)."""
    files = []
    for m in MISE_MARKERS:
        p = d / m
        if m.endswith(".toml") and p.is_file():
            files.append(p)
        elif m.endswith("conf.d") and p.is_dir():
            files.extend(sorted(f for f in p.glob("*.toml") if f.is_file()))
    return files


def read_mise_static(repo):
    """Task names from an untrusted mise config's TOML, running nothing: mise refuses to list a
    config with `[vars]` or a template until it is trusted, and listing a trusted one evaluates its
    `[vars]` templates, `exec` included. Same tuple shape as read_mise; see the module docstring for the limits."""
    root = repo.resolve()
    tasks = {}
    notes = []
    for f in mise_config_files(repo):
        try:
            data = tomllib.loads(f.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
            raise ReaderError(f"{f.relative_to(repo).as_posix()} unreadable: {e}")
        table = data.get("tasks", {})
        if not isinstance(table, dict):
            raise ReaderError(f"{f.relative_to(repo).as_posix()}: `tasks` is not a table")
        tc = data.get("task_config")
        if isinstance(tc, dict) and tc.get("includes"):
            notes.append(f"task_config.includes in {f.relative_to(repo).as_posix()} not followed")
        rel = f.relative_to(root).as_posix() if f.is_relative_to(root) else f.name
        for name, val in table.items():
            if isinstance(val, str):
                val = {"run": val}
            if not isinstance(val, dict):
                raise ReaderError(f"{rel}: task `{name}` is neither a string nor a table")
            run = val.get("run")
            runs = [run] if isinstance(run, str) else [x for x in (run or []) if isinstance(x, str)] \
                if isinstance(run, list) else []
            body = " ".join(runs)
            file_ = val.get("file")
            if isinstance(file_, str):
                try:
                    with open(repo / file_, "rb") as fh:
                        body = fh.read(MISE_BODY_MAX).decode("utf-8", "replace")
                except OSError:
                    body = ""
            shell_finding = None if file_ or not runs else mise_shell_finding(val.get("shell"))
            tasks[name] = (name, None, body, rel, shell_finding)
    if any((repo / m).is_dir() for m in MISE_TASK_DIRS):
        notes.append("file tasks (mise-tasks/ and its other spellings) not read")
    MISE_STATIC.append((repo, notes))
    return list(tasks.values())


def read_mise(repo):
    if not is_mise_dir(repo):
        return None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["mise", "tasks", "ls", "--json", "--hidden"], cwd=repo, capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=120, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise ReaderError("mise not found on PATH")
    if r.returncode != 0 and "are not trusted" in r.stderr:
        return read_mise_static(repo)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        err = [l.strip() for l in r.stderr.splitlines() if l.strip()]
        why = next((l for l in err if l.startswith("×")), err[-1] if err else f"rc={r.returncode}")
        raise ReaderError(f"mise tasks ls failed: {why}")
    try:
        listed = json.loads(r.stdout)
        if not isinstance(listed, list):
            raise ValueError("not a list")
    except ValueError as e:
        raise ReaderError(f"mise tasks ls --json gave no task list: {e}")
    root = repo.resolve()
    out = []
    for t in listed:
        try:
            name, src = t["name"], Path(t["source"]).resolve()
        except (KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            raise ReaderError(f"mise tasks ls --json: a task without a name or source ({e})")
        try:
            rel = src.relative_to(root)  # outside: a parent's or the global config's task
        except ValueError:
            continue
        run = [r for r in (t.get("run") or []) if isinstance(r, str)]
        body = " ".join(run)
        if t.get("file"):
            try:
                with open(t["file"], "rb") as f:
                    body = f.read(MISE_BODY_MAX).decode("utf-8", "replace")
            except OSError:
                body = ""
        shell_finding = None if t.get("file") or not run else mise_shell_finding(t.get("shell"))
        out.append((name, None, body, rel.as_posix(), shell_finding))
    return out


# The shells whose pipelines take `-o pipefail`. A task whose `shell` names another interpreter
# (`python3 -c`, `node -e`) has no pipelines to guard.
POSIX_SHELLS = {"sh", "bash", "zsh", "dash", "ksh", "mksh", "ash"}


def mise_shell_finding(shell):
    """Why an inline mise task's `shell` is refused, or None. mise ignores
    `unix_default_inline_shell_args` in a project's mise.toml, so an inline task that names no shell
    of its own runs without pipefail: `false | true` succeeds (measured with mise 2026.11.5)."""
    if not shell or not str(shell).strip():
        return "shell: names no `shell`, so mise runs it without pipefail; add `shell = \"bash -euo pipefail -c\"`"
    words = str(shell).split()
    if Path(words[0]).name in POSIX_SHELLS and "pipefail" not in words:
        return f"shell: `{shell}` does not set pipefail; add `-o pipefail`"
    return None


# (tool, reader, marker filenames) — markers None => root-only (nx: the workspace
# graph already covers nested projects; walking would double-count).
READERS = [
    ("just", read_just, ("justfile", "Justfile", ".justfile")),
    ("npm", read_npm, ("package.json",)),
    ("make", read_make, ("Makefile", "makefile", "GNUmakefile")),
    ("nx", read_nx, None),
    ("mise", read_mise, is_mise_dir),
]


# --- the fleet denominator -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Pointed at ~/Devel, this tool walks `external/` -- upstream clones and AUR build
# trees -- and lints task names in other people's code. Measured 2026-09-04 that is 1,569 of 1,588
# findings, none of them anybody's to rename here.
#
# It went unnoticed because a READER ERROR was hiding it: the make reader errored on a Go module
# cache inside an AUR build dir, and an errored reader has ALL its findings discarded. Pruning the
# cache fixed the error and the count went from 19 to 1,588 -- so the number being
# reasoned about was correct only by accident, and would have become wrong the moment the error was
# fixed. That is exactly why an errored reader must never be treated as a smaller clean run.
#
# The fix is to lint the FLEET (the repositories the mani manifest declares) rather than the
# filesystem. If the manifest is unreadable this REFUSES rather than falling back to
# a shorter list, for the same reason: the degraded reading is wrong in the direction that invites a
# false all-clear.
def manifest_refuse(manifest, lineno, line, why):
    print(f"lint-tasknames: manifest {manifest}:{lineno}: {why}: {line.strip()!r} -- refusing to guess "
          "which project this is", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


PROJECT_KEY = re.compile(r"""^  (?:([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)|"([^"]+)"|'([^']+)')\s*:\s*(?:#.*)?$""")
PROJECT_PATH = re.compile(r"""^    path:\s*(?:"([^"]+)"|'([^']+)'|([^\s#"']+))\s*(?:#.*)?$""")


def manifest_projects(manifest):
    """[(project name, absolute path, is_upstream)] from the manifest, in file order.

    The project NAME (the key under `projects:`) is what identifies a repository to an allowance:
    unique by construction (a YAML key), and the same on every machine, which an absolute path is not.
    An entry this reader cannot parse is refused (exit 2, naming the line) and never attributed to
    its neighbor: a `path:` that lands on the wrong project would lint one repository under another's
    allowances. Blank lines and comments, at any column, do not end the `projects:` section.
    """
    if not manifest.is_file():
        print(f"lint-tasknames: manifest unreadable: {manifest} -- refusing to lint a partial fleet",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    # Skip projects tagged `upstream` (callers decide): they are somebody else's source, recorded in the
    # manifest so the inventory is complete, and linting their task names measures nothing about this
    # fleet while burying the real figure. Measured: sweeping them turns ~76 findings into ~1590, almost
    # all from vendored build trees. NOT `external` -- that tag also covers our own AUR package repos.
    out, root, names = [], manifest.parent, set()
    cur = None  # [name, path, is_up, lineno]
    in_projects = False

    def flush():
        if cur is None:
            return
        name, path, is_up, lineno = cur
        if path is None:  # a seat, not a repository: nothing to lint, and it must not inherit a neighbor's path
            return
        out.append((name, path if path.is_absolute() else root / path, is_up))

    for n, line in enumerate(manifest.read_text().splitlines(), 1):
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        if not line[0].isspace():
            flush(); cur = None
            in_projects = re.match(r"^projects:\s*(#.*)?$", line) is not None
            continue
        if not in_projects:
            continue
        indent = len(line) - len(line.lstrip(" "))
        if indent < 4:
            m = PROJECT_KEY.match(line)
            if not m:
                manifest_refuse(manifest, n, line, "a project key this reader cannot parse")
            flush()
            name = next(g for g in m.groups() if g)
            if name in names:
                manifest_refuse(manifest, n, line, f"project '{name}' is defined twice")
            names.add(name)
            cur = [name, None, False, n]
        elif indent == 4:
            if cur is None:
                manifest_refuse(manifest, n, line, "a property outside any project")
            if line.startswith("    path:"):
                m = PROJECT_PATH.match(line)
                if not m or cur[1] is not None:
                    manifest_refuse(manifest, n, line, "a `path:` this reader cannot parse, or a second one")
                cur[1] = Path(next(g for g in m.groups() if g))
            elif line.startswith("    tags:") and "upstream" in line:
                cur[2] = True
    flush()
    return out


def fleet_paths(manifest):
    return [p for _, p, up in manifest_projects(manifest) if not up and p.is_dir()]


def git_roots(path):
    """(top level, main checkout) of the repository holding `path`, or (None, None).

    Plain `git` from PATH. A linked worktree's main checkout is the parent of the common git
    directory, so a worktree is matched to the project the manifest declares.
    """
    def g(*args):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path), *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return None
        return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() else None

    top = g("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if not top:
        return None, None
    main = Path(top)
    common = g("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    if common and Path(common).name == ".git":
        main = Path(common).parent
    return Path(top), main


def identify(repo, manifest):
    """(project key or None, why not, top directory) for the repository being linted."""
    top, main = git_roots(repo)
    base = top.resolve() if top else repo
    try:
        repo.relative_to(base)
    except ValueError:
        base = repo
    if not manifest.is_file():
        return None, f"the manifest could not be read ({manifest})", base
    wanted = [x.resolve() for x in (main, top) if x is not None] or [repo.resolve()]
    projects = [(n, p.resolve()) for n, p, _ in manifest_projects(manifest) if p.is_dir()]
    for w in wanted:
        hits = sorted({n for n, p in projects if p == w})
        if len(hits) == 1:
            return hits[0], "", base
        if hits:
            return None, f"manifest keys {' and '.join(hits)} share this repository's path", base
    return None, f"this repository is not in the manifest ({manifest})", base


# --- the commit-gate mode ------------------------------------------------------------------------

def staged_path_triggers(rel):
    """True when the repo-relative path `rel` is a file one of the readers parses, derived from the
    readers' own constants: the just/npm/make marker file names (any depth, like the walk), `nx.json`
    at the root (the workspace is root-only), an Nx `project.json` at any depth, the mise config spellings and task directories in MISE_MARKERS
    (the file itself, or anything under a marker directory), never a path inside a directory the
    walk skips (EXCLUDE_DIRS, fixture directories)."""
    parts = Path(rel).parts
    if not parts:
        return False
    if any(d in EXCLUDE_DIRS or is_fixture_dir(d) for d in parts[:-1]):
        return False
    if len(parts) == 1 and parts[0] == NX_MARKER:
        return True
    if parts[-1] == NX_PROJECT_FILE:
        return True
    for _tool, _reader, markers in READERS:
        if isinstance(markers, tuple) and parts[-1] in markers:
            return True
    for m in set(MISE_MARKERS) | set(MISE_TASK_DIRS):
        mp = Path(m).parts
        n = len(mp)
        for i in range(len(parts) - n + 1):
            if parts[i:i + n] == mp:
                return True  # the marker file itself, or a file inside a marker directory
    return False


def run_staged(argv):
    """`--staged [REPO]`: lint REPO's working tree only when a staged path is a task file."""
    if "--fleet" in argv:
        print("lint-tasknames: --staged cannot be combined with --fleet", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if len(argv) > 1:
        print("lint-tasknames: usage: lint-tasknames --staged [REPO]", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    start = Path(argv[0] if argv else ".")
    if not start.is_dir():
        print(f"lint-tasknames: not a directory: {start}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        top = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(start), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True,
                             text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        if top.returncode != 0:
            print(f"lint-tasknames: --staged: {start} is not in a git repository", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        repo = Path(top.stdout.strip()).resolve()
        lst = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=ACMR", "-z"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"lint-tasknames: --staged: git failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if lst.returncode != 0:
        print(f"lint-tasknames: --staged: git diff --cached failed: {lst.stderr.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not any(staged_path_triggers(p) for p in lst.stdout.split("\0") if p):
        print("lint-tasknames: no task file staged")
        return 0
    rc = lint_target(repo)
    if rc:
        print("lint-tasknames: commit refused. The vocabulary is skill `naming-build-tasks`.")
    return rc


def main():
    global STRICT
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--strict" in argv:
        STRICT = True
        argv = [a for a in argv if a != "--strict"]
    env_number("LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_TIMEOUT", SURFACE_TIMEOUT)
    env_number("LINT_TASKNAMES_SURFACE_MAX_BYTES", SURFACE_MAX_BYTES)
    load_allowances()
    env_manifest = os.environ.get("LINT_TASKNAMES_MANIFEST")
    if env_manifest and not Path(env_manifest).is_file():
        print(f"lint-tasknames: manifest unreadable: {env_manifest} (from LINT_TASKNAMES_MANIFEST)",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if "--staged" in argv:
        return run_staged([a for a in argv if a != "--staged"])
    # A usage error is exit 2, never a lint result: an unknown option or a path that is not a
    # directory once printed "clean." and exited 0, so a mistyped path read as a clean repository.
    unknown = [a for a in argv if a.startswith("-") and a != "--fleet"]
    if unknown:
        print(f"lint-tasknames: unknown option {unknown[0]}; usage: lint-tasknames [--strict] "
              "[REPO | --fleet [MANIFEST] | --staged [REPO]]", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if argv and argv[0] != "--fleet" and not Path(argv[0]).is_dir():
        print(f"lint-tasknames: not a directory: {argv[0]}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if argv and argv[0] == "--fleet":
        manifest = Path(argv[1]) if len(argv) > 1 else Path(env_manifest) if env_manifest else DEFAULT_MANIFEST
        rc, seen = 0, set()
        projects = manifest_projects(manifest)
        for name, p, up in projects:
            if up or not p.is_dir():
                continue
            seen.add(name)
            rc = max(rc, lint_one(p, key=name))
        # An allowance naming a repository the fleet does not have is stale in the same sense as one
        # naming a task that no longer exists, and nothing else would ever report it.
        if ALLOWANCES:
            known = {n for n, _, _ in projects}
            for a in ALLOWANCES[1]:
                if a["repo"] not in seen:
                    why = "is tagged upstream or missing on disk" if a["repo"] in known else "is not in the manifest"
                    print(f"  WARN  [{a['_label']}] repo '{a['repo']}' {why}: stale allowance")
                    if STRICT:
                        rc = max(rc, 1)
        return rc
    return lint_target(Path(argv[0] if argv else ".").resolve())


def lint_target(repo):
    """Lint one repository directory, attributing allowances through the manifest when there are any."""
    env_manifest = os.environ.get("LINT_TASKNAMES_MANIFEST")
    manifest = Path(env_manifest) if env_manifest else DEFAULT_MANIFEST
    if ALLOWANCES is None:  # nothing to attribute: do not read the manifest at all
        return lint_one(repo)
    key, why, base = identify(repo, manifest)
    return lint_one(repo, key=key, why=why, base=base)


def lint_one(repo, key=None, why="", base=None):
    base = base or repo
    SKIPPED_FIXTURES.clear()
    MISE_STATIC.clear()
    categories, skill = load_categories()

    violations, tally, reader_errors, all_names = [], [], [], []
    mine = [a for a in ALLOWANCES[1] if key is not None and a["repo"] == key] if ALLOWANCES else []
    for tool, reader, markers in READERS:
        dirs = [repo] if markers is None else find_dirs(repo, markers, MISE_OWN_DIRS if tool == "mise" else ())
        tasks, errored, applicable = [], False, 0
        for d in dirs:
            rel = None if d == repo else str(d.relative_to(repo))
            dr = d.relative_to(base).as_posix()
            if markers is None:
                bfile = NX_MARKER  # Nx has no build file per project; allowances use the workspace file
            else:
                bfile = "mise.toml" if callable(markers) else real_build_file(d, markers)
                bfile = bfile if dr == "." else f"{dr}/{bfile}"
            try:
                found = reader(d)
            except ReaderError as e:
                loc = rel or "."
                reader_errors.append((tool, f"[{loc}] {e}"))
                errored = True
                continue
            if found is None:
                continue
            applicable += 1
            for item in found:
                name, ctx = item[0], item[1]
                body = item[2] if len(item) > 2 else ""
                # A reader that knows each task's own file (mise: `mise.toml` or a task file) names it,
                # relative to the directory read, so an allowance's `path` can say which.
                own = item[3] if len(item) > 3 else None
                shell_finding = item[4] if len(item) > 4 else None
                tfile = bfile if own is None else (own if dr == "." else f"{dr}/{own}")
                tasks.append((name, ctx if ctx is not None else rel, body, tfile, shell_finding))
        if errored:
            tally.append(f"{tool}(ERROR)")
            continue
        if applicable == 0:
            tally.append(f"{tool}(-)")
            continue
        tally.append(f"{tool}({len(tasks)})" + (f"@{len(dirs)}d" if len(dirs) > 1 else ""))
        for name, ctx, body, bfile, shell_finding in tasks:
            if tool == "just" and name in JUST_IDIOM:
                continue
            if tool == "nx" and name in NX_PROVIDED:
                continue
            if tool == "mise" and name in MISE_IDIOM:
                continue
            all_names.append((name, bfile))
            where = f"{tool}:{ctx}" if ctx else tool
            if ":" in name:
                violations.append((ERROR, where, name, "separator: colon (the shape is verb-qualifier, hyphen)"))
                head = name.split(":")[0]
            else:
                head = name.split("-")[0]
            allowed = [a for a in mine if name in a["names"] and a.get("path", bfile) == bfile]
            if allowed:
                a = allowed[0]
                violations.append((INFO, where, name, f"allowed: mirrors {a['mirror']} ({a['reason']})"))
            elif head not in categories:
                violations.append((WARN, where, name, f"verb: leading token '{head}' is not a vocabulary category"))
            t = triad_finding(name, body)
            if t:
                violations.append((ERROR, where, name, t))
            if shell_finding:
                violations.append((ERROR, where, name, shell_finding))

    # Stale allowances: a claimed name no current task carries. Skipped when a reader errored, because
    # the task list is then incomplete and "no such task" would be a guess.
    if not reader_errors and base == repo:
        present = set(all_names)
        for a in mine:
            for n in a["names"]:
                if not any(nm == n and a.get("path", bf) == bf for nm, bf in present):
                    where_ = a.get("path", "the repository")
                    violations.append((WARN, a["_label"], n, f"stale allowance: no task of that name in {where_} ({repo.name})"))
    # Surface verification runs only under --strict: it executes a command, so a default run stays read-only.
    if STRICT:
        for a in mine:
            for reason in verify_surface(a, base):
                violations.append((ERROR, a["_label"], "surface", reason))
    if ALLOWANCES is None:
        astate = f"allowances: none (no file at {ALLOWANCES_LOOKED_AT})"
    elif key is None:
        astate = f"allowances: {len(ALLOWANCES[1])} in {ALLOWANCES[0]}, but {why}, so none apply"
    else:
        astate = (f"allowances: {len(mine)} for {key} ({len(ALLOWANCES[1])} in {ALLOWANCES[0]}); "
                  + ("surfaces run (--strict)" if STRICT else "surfaces NOT run (no --strict)")
                  + ("" if base == repo else "; run from a subdirectory, so the stale check is skipped"))
    print(f"lint-tasknames: {repo.name}  sources: {' '.join(tally)}  (vocabulary: {skill})")
    print(f"  {astate}")
    if MISE_STATIC:
        shown = ", ".join(("." if d == repo else str(d.relative_to(repo))) for d, _ in MISE_STATIC)
        print(f"  mise: {len(MISE_STATIC)} director{'y' if len(MISE_STATIC) == 1 else 'ies'} untrusted, "
              f"read without mise (static TOML read, nothing run): {shown}")
        for d, notes in MISE_STATIC:
            for n in notes:
                print(f"  mise: not covered in {'.' if d == repo else d.relative_to(repo)}: {n}")
    if SKIPPED_FIXTURES:
        rels = sorted(str(d.relative_to(repo)) for d in SKIPPED_FIXTURES)
        shown = ", ".join(rels[:3]) + (f" and {len(rels) - 3} more" if len(rels) > 3 else "")
        print(f"  skipped: {len(rels)} fixture director{'y' if len(rels) == 1 else 'ies'}, test input "
              f"rather than this repository's tasks: {shown}")
    # ERROR first: severity ordering is the whole point of the split, and a reader who stops after
    # the first few lines should have seen the findings that actually mean something.
    order = {ERROR: 0, WARN: 1, INFO: 2}
    for sev, where, name, reason in sorted(violations, key=lambda v: order[v[0]]):
        print(f"  {sev:<5} [{where}] {name}: {reason}")
    for tool, msg in reader_errors:
        print(f"  READER ERROR [{tool}]: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)

    n_err = sum(1 for v in violations if v[0] == ERROR)
    n_warn = sum(1 for v in violations if v[0] == WARN)
    n_info = sum(1 for v in violations if v[0] == INFO)

    if reader_errors:
        print(f"{n_err} ERROR, {n_warn} WARN; {len(reader_errors)} READER ERROR(S) — "
              "coverage is incomplete, this run proves nothing for those sources", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if violations:
        print(f"{n_err} ERROR, {n_warn} WARN, {n_info} INFO.")
    else:
        print("clean.")
    # Gates on ERROR only: a leading token outside the vocabulary is worth SEEING,
    # never worth blocking a commit over. --strict also fails on WARN.
    if n_err:
        return 1
    return 1 if (n_warn and STRICT) else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
